100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (Without precise, product-wise sales tracking, slow-moving garment categories like premium sarees or seasonal suit pieces remain on shelves significantly longer than anticipated. This stagnation causes a substantial amount of the store's working capital to become locked up in inactive inventory, creating artificial liquidity constraints. Since the business currently lacks a data-backed discounting or promotional mechanism to systematically liquidate this slow-moving stock, managing everyday cash flow becomes inefficient. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (External Problems)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (The garment retail sector experiences seasonal demand fluctuations, making it difficult for small retailers to anticipate customer buying patterns without structured data. Competitive pressure in the local Muzaffarpur market further demands that the store maintain a wide product variety, which increases the risk of overstocking low-demand items while understocking high-demand categories.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (3.1 Reducing Locked Working Capital: Matching Point lacks a structured data framework to track category-wise sales performance. Consequently, slow-moving garment stock remains undetected on shelves for long periods, leading to locked working capital and artificial liquidity constraints for operating costs.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (3.2 Minimizing Total Inventory Cost: Stocking and procurement decisions are heavily dependent on manual intuition and memory rather than quantitative analysis. This lack of demand visibility causes structural inventory imbalances, creating overstocking in low-demand lines \(which increases holding costs\) and frequent stockouts in high-demand items \(which results in lost sales revenue\).)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
