<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (ABC Analysis: This quantitative method will be used to classify product categories based on their revenue contribution. The rationale is to objectively separate key revenue-generating items from underperforming ones to aid capital reallocation.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Inventory Turnover Analysis: This method will be applied to calculate the velocity of stock cycles across product groups. The rationale is to isolate specific low-turnover garment categories that are contributing the most to stagnant capital.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (For Problem 2 \(Minimizing Total Inventory Cost\):)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (Descriptive Statistical Analysis: This method will be used to analyze parameters like mean and variance in purchasing distributions. The rationale is to rank categories based on consistent frequency and identify variation across trading periods.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 205 0 R
/E (Moving Average-based Demand Forecasting: This time-series statistical technique will be used to smooth random variations in transactional records. The rationale is to estimate near-future demand trends to drive a structured procurement cycle.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Store metadata such as operational timings, staff count, and estimated annual revenue will also be recorded to provide overall business context. Primary data collection was chosen over secondary sources because no external dataset can accurately capture Matching Point's specific sales and demand behavior; structured day-wise records ensure the reliability required for meaningful quantitative analysis of both problems.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (The data collection process will focus on three main areas relevant to both problem statements: sales, purchase/procurement, and store operations. Day-wise sales data, including transaction date, product category, quantity sold, and selling price per unit, will be collected from daily sales bills and physical registers maintained by the store, since digital records are not kept. This data will primarily support the ABC and turnover analysis for Problem 1, as well as the demand pattern analysis for Problem 2. Purchase and procurement data, including cost price per category, order dates, and stock replenishment frequency, will additionally be gathered to support the turnover and cost-related aspects of Problem 1. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
